  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Flood Response Support in Mayendit County, South Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Objective: 
To provide life-saving assistance to 6,000 flood affected population  in Mayendit County, South Sudan.
The total reach is 6,000 individuals (1,000 HH). While the WASH intervention alone reaches 6,000 individuals, the NFI and Health vaccine activities may overlap with the same population. To avoid double-counting, we typically report the highest unique reach across sectors unless disaggregated data confirms distinct beneficiaries. This is in alignment with common humanitarian reporting standards to ensure conservative, non-duplicative beneficiary estimates.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Sudan is experiencing catastrophic flooding, with more than 350,000 people displaced across the country. Unity State is the epicenter of the crisis, with Panyijar, Koch, Leer, and Mayendit counties accounting for over 200,000 displaced households.
The Ministry of Water Resources and Irrigation continues real-time river monitoring. As of 8 September 2025, water levels in Juba have slightly decreased but remain high in Mangala, where moderate flooding is expected. Localized increases have also been reported in Malakal, Anakdiar (Sobat River), and Nyamlel (Aweil West).
Above-normal rainfall is forecast from July to November 2025, placing 1.6 million people at risk. Peak flooding is expected between September and December, driven by upstream water releases from Uganda and Ethiopia. High-risk areas include Jonglei, Upper Nile, Unity, Warrap, Lakes, Northern Bahr el Ghazal, and parts of Central Equatoria.
Humanitarian partners are actively responding:
WFP has launched food airdrops and distributions for over 39,000 people in Fangak Town and Paguir.
A national NGO has provided WASH supplies to 150 households in Paguir Payam.
South Sudan Red Cross will distribute NFIs to 800 households and cash assistance to 1,000 households, while engaging 1,200 youth in dyke construction through a Cash-for-Work program.
Mercy Corps is conducting drainage works in Rotriak, Rubkona County.
Despite these efforts, access remains a major challenge due to impassable roads and submerged communities. Funding shortfalls are also hampering response efforts. The 2025 Humanitarian Needs and Response Plan is only 29.2% funded, with $494.7 million received out of the $1.7 billion appeal.
Flood Task Forces at national and sub-national levels have been activated to coordinate preparedness and mitigation efforts. Urgent support is needed to scale up the response and prevent further humanitarian impact.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is inclusive and will offer service to all ages and genders affected by flooding and other climate change factors  Male +Female,Girls + Boys including Elderly and person with disabilities</narrative>
  </description>
